# XSH setreuid()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Appropriate privileges are implementation-defined (XBD 3.21). Each test
# runs in a child of a privileged test process and first probes, in a
# disposable process and the setup phase, a call that only a privileged
# process may make; the test is UNRESOLVED when the probe does not show
# the state the assertion needs. The saved set-user-ID is observed through
# seteuid(): without privileges it accepts only the real user ID and the
# saved set-user-ID. User IDs used: 65534 and 65533 (given to the
# process), 65532 (probes), 65531 (refused targets).

assertion  XSH.setreuid.001
anchor     functions/setreuid.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/setreuid.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       With appropriate privileges, setreuid(65534, 65533) returns 0,
           getuid() is then 65534 and geteuid() 65533, and the saved
           set-user-ID is 65533: once no privilege is left, seteuid(65534)
           and then seteuid(65533) both work.
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/001-privileged.c

assertion  XSH.setreuid.002
anchor     functions/setreuid.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With appropriate privileges, setreuid(-1, 65533) changes only the
           effective user ID (getuid() stays 0) and setreuid(65534, -1)
           only the real user ID (geteuid() stays 0).
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/002-minus-one.c

assertion  XSH.setreuid.003
anchor     functions/setreuid.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A process without appropriate privileges with real user ID 65534
           and effective and saved set-user-ID 65533 may set its effective
           user ID to the real one, setreuid(-1, 65534), and then back to
           the saved one, setreuid(-1, 65533): both return 0 and geteuid()
           follows, getuid() staying 65534.
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/003-unprivileged.c

assertion  XSH.setreuid.004
anchor     functions/setreuid.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      2008
says       After setreuid(-1, 65534) in that process (the real user ID
           left alone and the effective one set to the real one), the
           saved set-user-ID is still 65533: seteuid(65533) works.
note       disputed (Q-69): the page says when the saved set-user-ID is set
           to the new effective ID but not, in normative text, that it is
           otherwise left alone (setregid()'s APPLICATION USAGE says so for
           groups). A FAIL is logged as INSPECT.
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/004-saved-kept.c

assertion  XSH.setreuid.005
anchor     functions/setreuid.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       The saved set-user-ID becomes the new effective ID when the real
           ID is set: from 65534/65533/65533 without privileges,
           setreuid(65534, 65534) leaves seteuid(65533) failing with EPERM;
           and when the effective ID is set to a value other than the real
           one: after setreuid(65534, -1) and setreuid(-1, 65533) with
           privileges, seteuid(0) fails with EPERM once no privilege is
           left.
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/005-saved-set.c

assertion  XSH.setreuid.006
anchor     functions/setreuid.html#ERRORS/EPERM
anchor     functions/setreuid.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       A process without appropriate privileges with real user ID 65534
           and effective and saved set-user-ID 65533 gets -1 with EPERM
           from setreuid(-1, 65531), and its IDs are unchanged.
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/006-eperm.c

assertion  XSH.setreuid.007
anchor     functions/setreuid.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9
class      required
since      2001
says       When one thread of a privileged process calls setreuid(65534,
           65533), another thread of the process then gets 65534 from
           getuid() and 65533 from geteuid().
needs      root
nomutant   no fault is planted in a call that changes the process IDs (reviewed by hand against the text; see docs/methodology.md)
test       t/007-threads.c
